%%%
# Cumulative-logit ordinal regression: cheese tasting

Fifty-two judges rated each of four cheese additives A to D on a nine-point
scale, from 1 (strong dislike) to 9 (excellent taste). The counts are the
two-way table of McCullagh and Nelder, *Generalized Linear Models* (1989),
from McCullagh (1980).

The cumulative-logit model puts eight increasing cut points
$c_1 < \dots < c_8$ on the logit scale and gives additive $a$ a location
$\eta_a$:

$$\Pr(Y \le j \mid a) = \operatorname{invlogit}(c_j - \eta_a), \qquad j = 1, \dots, 8.$$

A rating's probability is the difference of two neighbouring cumulative
probabilities, with $\Pr(Y \le 0) = 0$ and $\Pr(Y \le 9) = 1$. The cut points
are declared on `ordered(8)`, the set of strictly increasing real 8-vectors.
Additive D is the reference category, $\eta_D = 0$, so `effect` holds
$\eta_A, \eta_B, \eta_C$. Each additive's counts are one multinomial draw of
its 52 ratings.
%%%
flatppl_compat = "0.1"

# Ratings 1 to 9 per additive, 52 judges each.
counts_A = [0, 0, 1, 7, 8, 8, 19, 8, 1]
counts_B = [6, 9, 12, 11, 7, 6, 1, 0, 0]
counts_C = [1, 1, 6, 8, 23, 7, 5, 1, 0]
counts_D = [0, 0, 0, 1, 3, 7, 14, 16, 11]

% Cut points on the logit scale
cuts = elementof(ordered(8))
% Locations of additives A, B and C relative to D
effect = elementof(cartpow(reals, 3))

% Locations of additives A, B, C and D
eta = cat(effect, [0.0])

# Cumulative probabilities of ratings 1 to 8 per additive.
F_A = invlogit.(cuts .- eta[1])
F_B = invlogit.(cuts .- eta[2])
F_C = invlogit.(cuts .- eta[3])
F_D = invlogit.(cuts .- eta[4])

# Rating probabilities: differences of neighbouring cumulative probabilities.
y_A ~ Multinomial(n = 52, p = cat(F_A, [1.0]) .- cat([0.0], F_A))
y_B ~ Multinomial(n = 52, p = cat(F_B, [1.0]) .- cat([0.0], F_B))
y_C ~ Multinomial(n = 52, p = cat(F_C, [1.0]) .- cat([0.0], F_C))
y_D ~ Multinomial(n = 52, p = cat(F_D, [1.0]) .- cat([0.0], F_D))

K_model = kernelof(record(y_A = y_A, y_B = y_B, y_C = y_C, y_D = y_D)) % Forward kernel (observation model)

L = likelihoodof(K_model, record(y_A = counts_A, y_B = counts_B, y_C = counts_C, y_D = counts_D))

% Point preset near the maximum-likelihood fit
L_input = record(
    cuts   = [-7.08, -6.02, -4.93, -3.86, -2.52, -1.57, -0.07, 1.49],
    effect = [-1.61, -4.96, -3.32]
)

% Log-likelihood at the preset point
logL_input = logdensityof(L, L_input)
